{"name":"Bottle Glass","hex":"#238e50","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bottle","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A container, typically made of glass or plastic and having a tapered neck, used primarily for holding liquids.","The contents of such a container."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To seal (a liquid) into a bottle for later consumption. Also fig.","To feed (an infant) baby formula."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bottle"},{"term":"glass","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An amorphous solid, often transparent substance, usually made by melting silica sand with various additives (for most purposes, a mixture of soda, potash and lime is added).","Any amorphous solid (one without a regular crystal lattice)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To fit with glass; to glaze.","To enclose in glass."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glass"}],"entries":[{"title":"Glass production","blocks":[{"text":"Glass production involves two main methods – the float glass process that produces sheet glass, and glassblowing that produces bottles and other containers. It has been done in a variety of ways during the history of glass."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glass_production","wikidata":"Q785222"},{"title":"Bottle","blocks":[{"text":"A bottle is a narrow-necked container made of an impermeable material in various shapes and sizes that stores and transports liquids. Its mouth, at the bottling line, can be sealed with an internal stopper, an external bottle cap, a closure, or induction sealing."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Bouteille.jpg/330px-Bouteille.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bottle","wikidata":"Q80228"},{"title":"Glass","blocks":[{"text":"Glass is an amorphous (non-crystalline) solid. Because it is often transparent and chemically inert, glass has found widespread practical, technological, and decorative use in window panes, tableware, and optics. Some common objects made of glass are named after the material, e.g., a \"glass\" for drinking, \"glasses\" for vision correction, and a \"magnifying glass\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Fassade_Wilhelmstrasse_65%2C_Berlin-Mitte%2C_160417%2C_ako.jpg/330px-Fassade_Wilhelmstrasse_65%2C_Berlin-Mitte%2C_160417%2C_ako.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glass","wikidata":"Q11469"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/THE_INTERIOR_OF_THE_2500_DEGREE_F._FURNACE_WHERE_CRUSHED_BOTTLES_ARE_MADE_INTO_MOLTEN_GLASS._PART_OF_A_NEW_GLASS..._-_NARA_-_543707.jpg/500px-THE_INTERIOR_OF_THE_2500_DEGREE_F._FURNACE_WHERE_CRUSHED_BOTTLES_ARE_MADE_INTO_MOLTEN_GLASS._PART_OF_A_NEW_GLASS..._-_NARA_-_543707.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/THE_INTERIOR_OF_THE_2500_DEGREE_F._FURNACE_WHERE_CRUSHED_BOTTLES_ARE_MADE_INTO_MOLTEN_GLASS._PART_OF_A_NEW_GLASS..._-_NARA_-_543707.jpg/1280px-THE_INTERIOR_OF_THE_2500_DEGREE_F._FURNACE_WHERE_CRUSHED_BOTTLES_ARE_MADE_INTO_MOLTEN_GLASS._PART_OF_A_NEW_GLASS..._-_NARA_-_543707.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/THE_INTERIOR_OF_THE_2500_DEGREE_F._FURNACE_WHERE_CRUSHED_BOTTLES_ARE_MADE_INTO_MOLTEN_GLASS._PART_OF_A_NEW_GLASS..._-_NARA_-_543707.jpg/500px-THE_INTERIOR_OF_THE_2500_DEGREE_F._FURNACE_WHERE_CRUSHED_BOTTLES_ARE_MADE_INTO_MOLTEN_GLASS._PART_OF_A_NEW_GLASS..._-_NARA_-_543707.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Batch feed doghouse of a glass furnace","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATHE_INTERIOR_OF_THE_2500_DEGREE_F._FURNACE_WHERE_CRUSHED_BOTTLES_ARE_MA